# Complexity

How the cost of a sort is described, and why the description is deliberately
imprecise.

## The notation

Three symbols do almost all the work. Each bounds a function $f(n)$ — the count
of operations on an input of size $n$ — by a simpler function $g(n)$:

- $O(g)$ — an **upper** bound. $f$ grows no faster than $g$, up to a constant.
- $\Omega(g)$ — a **lower** bound. $f$ grows at least as fast as $g$.
- $\Theta(g)$ — both at once. $f$ grows exactly like $g$.

Formally, $f(n) = O(g(n))$ when there are constants $c > 0$ and $n_0$ such that

$$
0 \le f(n) \le c \cdot g(n) \quad \text{for all } n \ge n_0
$$

The constants are what the notation throws away, and throwing them away is the
point: they depend on the machine, the compiler, and the memory hierarchy, none
of which the algorithm controls. ^big-o

!!! warning "What the constants hide"

    [[insertion-sort]] is $O(n^2)$ and [[merge-sort]] is $O(n\log n)$, yet
    insertion sort is faster below roughly 10–50 elements on real hardware.
    Every production sort exploits this by switching to insertion sort on small
    partitions. An asymptotic bound describes the shape of a curve, not its
    height.

## The three cases

The same algorithm has three costs, and quoting only one is how people mislead
themselves:

- **Best case** — the input that costs least. Usually already-sorted data.
- **Average case** — the expectation over all input orderings, assumed uniform.
- **Worst case** — the input that costs most. The only bound you can promise.

[[quicksort]] is the sharp example: $\Theta(n\log n)$ on average and
$\Theta(n^2)$ in the worst case, and the worst case used to be *sorted input*
before randomised pivots became standard.

## Counting what matters

For a comparison sort, two operations are counted:

- **Comparisons** — asking whether $a_i < a_j$.
- **Swaps or moves** — writing an element to a new position.

They are not interchangeable. [[heapsort]] and [[merge-sort]] make a similar
number of comparisons, but merge sort moves every element $\log n$ times while
heapsort's moves are scattered — which is why merge sort is often faster in
practice despite using more memory. When a comparison is expensive, count
comparisons; when a move is expensive, count moves.

## The lower bound

Any comparison sort needs $\Omega(n\log n)$ comparisons in the worst case. A
sort's decision tree must have one leaf per possible ordering, so it has at
least $n!$ leaves; a binary tree with $n!$ leaves has depth at least
$\log_2(n!)$, and by Stirling's approximation $\log_2(n!) = \Theta(n\log n)$.
No cleverness in comparison-based sorting escapes it. ^lower-bound

??? tip "Sorts that beat the bound"

    Counting sort, radix sort, and bucket sort run in $O(n)$ or $O(n + k)$ —
    because they never compare two elements. They read the keys' structure
    instead, which is why they need to know something about the keys in advance.
    The lower bound applies to comparison sorts, and only to them.

## Recurrences

A divide-and-conquer sort's cost is naturally written as a recurrence. Merge
sort splits into two halves and merges in linear time:

$$
T(n) = 2\,T\!\left(\frac{n}{2}\right) + \Theta(n)
$$

The master theorem gives $T(n) = \Theta(n\log n)$. The same recurrence with an
unbalanced split — $T(n) = T(n-1) + \Theta(n)$ — gives $\Theta(n^2)$, which is
exactly quicksort's worst case.
